1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:03,468
Here's how to reverse engineer Kissflow from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,572 --> 00:00:07,748
We gave Claude one Markdown file, and checked twenty things in a real browser.

3
00:00:07,852 --> 00:00:09,699
Here's what passed, and what broke.

4
00:00:10,533 --> 00:00:14,906
The spec copies the part of Kissflow a small team uses for spend approvals.

5
00:00:15,015 --> 00:00:16,204
A short request form.

6
00:00:16,313 --> 00:00:18,445
Routing rules by amount and category.

7
00:00:18,554 --> 00:00:21,747
A chain of approvers who approve, reject, or send back.

8
00:00:21,856 --> 00:00:23,988
And an audit log that's never edited.

9
00:00:24,833 --> 00:00:26,186
The core rule is routing.

10
00:00:26,293 --> 00:00:31,181
Every request goes to the requester's manager first, then each rule is checked in order.

11
00:00:31,288 --> 00:00:38,365
Jo's twenty-four hundred dollar laptop goes to Maya, her manager, then to Ana in Finance, because it's over a thousand dollars.

12
00:00:38,471 --> 00:00:40,554
And nobody approves their own request.

13
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:44,819
When Ana asks, her Finance step is skipped, and her manager, Dana, decides.

14
00:00:45,667 --> 00:00:48,458
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

15
00:00:48,567 --> 00:00:51,239
The foundation, with people and routing rules.

16
00:00:51,348 --> 00:00:53,192
Requests, routing and decisions.

17
00:00:53,302 --> 00:00:55,501
Notifications, and approvals in Slack.

18
00:00:55,610 --> 00:00:58,756
Then delegation, reminders, escalation and the export.

19
00:00:58,865 --> 00:01:02,544
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser.

20
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:04,511
Here's what it built.

21
00:01:04,617 --> 00:01:08,050
The request form previews the approval chain before you submit.

22
00:01:08,156 --> 00:01:12,087
Each request shows the chain so far, with the approver's decision panel.

23
00:01:12,192 --> 00:01:15,792
The approvals inbox lists everything waiting on you, oldest first.

24
00:01:15,898 --> 00:01:20,382
And admins edit the routing rules, with a live example of where a request will go.

25
00:01:21,233 --> 00:01:26,956
In the final run, on a fresh database, all twenty checks passed, along with thirty-eight automated tests.

26
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:29,960
But the more useful result is what broke.

27
00:01:30,064 --> 00:01:32,170
The build exposed nine gaps in the spec.

28
00:01:32,274 --> 00:01:34,003
Places where Claude had to guess.

29
00:01:34,107 --> 00:01:39,717
Escalation could hand a step to someone who owned a later step, so they'd approve the same request twice.

30
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:43,166
The people screen allowed reporting loops, which break routing.

31
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:45,322
And Slack showed approval times in UTC.

32
00:01:45,426 --> 00:01:47,263
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

33
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:51,244
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

34
00:01:51,352 --> 00:01:55,891
We didn't test real sign-in, a live Slack workspace, sending email, or hosting.

35
00:01:55,999 --> 00:01:58,969
Those are yours to check before a team relies on it.

36
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:01,074
So is it worth building?

37
00:02:01,178 --> 00:02:02,849
Kissflow doesn't publish prices.

38
00:02:02,953 --> 00:02:05,969
You ask for a quote, and agreements are fixed and annual.

39
00:02:06,072 --> 00:02:11,939
If you need a no-code workflow designer, parallel approvals or ERP integrations, Kissflow is worth paying for.

40
00:02:12,042 --> 00:02:18,016
If your team just needs spend approvals, you mostly need the form, the rules, and a record of who approved what.

41
00:02:18,867 --> 00:02:19,808
The spec is free.

42
00:02:19,916 --> 00:02:26,688
The data model, thirteen rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all twenty checks, with the nine fixes already in.

43
00:02:26,796 --> 00:02:29,370
Find it at personal software factory dot com.
